[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083280,
      "id": "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "02b3b87c-005a-5ecd-8d40-871b5068b99e",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos(4t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6})+5,\\,y=6\\sin(4t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6})-3,\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t<\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-24\\sin(4t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=24\\cos(4t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n24\\cos(4t-\\frac{5\\pi}{6})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}) & =24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}) & =-24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{13\\pi}{12}) & =24\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}) & =-24\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{13\\pi}{12}, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "44d31767-e854-5ced-8016-ef1579c956da",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
