    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b7cf3bc9-8650-5cba-8801-495f7bfb77f3",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})+3,\\,y=6\\sin(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})-5,\\,\\frac{3\\pi}{5}\\le t<\\frac{7\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-20\\sin(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=30\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n30\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{19\\pi}{20}, \\frac{23\\pi}{20}, \\frac{27\\pi}{20}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}) & =20\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{19\\pi}{20}) & =-20\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{23\\pi}{20}) & =20\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{27\\pi}{20}) & =-20\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\frac{19\\pi}{20}, \\frac{23\\pi}{20}, \\frac{27\\pi}{20}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
