  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083280,
      "id": "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5db9a86c-a3d5-5d70-b38d-fa86e826a14e",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})+4,\\,y=4\\sin(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})-3,\\,\\frac{4\\pi}{5}\\le t<\\frac{8\\pi}{5}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers in ascending order.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}, $t=$\n{{field-2}}, $t=$\n{{field-3}}, $t=$\n{{field-4}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-10\\sin(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=20\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20\\cos(5t-\\frac{\\pi}{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{19\\pi}{20}, \\frac{23\\pi}{20}, \\frac{27\\pi}{20}, \\frac{31\\pi}{20}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{19\\pi}{20}) & =-10\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{23\\pi}{20}) & =10\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{27\\pi}{20}) & =-10\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{31\\pi}{20}) & =10\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{19\\pi}{20}, \\frac{23\\pi}{20}, \\frac{27\\pi}{20}, \\frac{31\\pi}{20}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "edbb4d87-e9bf-5dd3-b320-943b370444f7",
